        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in Albany City SD: A Comprehensive Guide for Parents</h1>

  <p>Albany City School District is committed to providing a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. This commitment extends to ensuring that disciplinary actions are fair, consistent, and in compliance with New York State Education Law and the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). Understanding your rights and the disciplinary procedures is crucial, especially if your child has an IEP or 504 plan. This guide provides a detailed overview of student discipline, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process rights within the Albany City School District.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Student Discipline in Albany City SD</h2>

  <p>Disciplinary actions in Albany City SD range from minor classroom interventions to more serious consequences like suspension and expulsion. It's important to understand the district's Code of Conduct, which outlines prohibited behaviors and associated disciplinary responses. You can typically find this document on the district's website (search "Albany City SD Code of Conduct"). Familiarize yourself with this document.</p>

  <p><b>Types of Disciplinary Actions:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Verbal Warning:</b> A direct reprimand to the student regarding inappropriate behavior.</li>
    <li><b>Written Warning:</b> A formal written notice of the student's misbehavior, often kept on file.</li>
    <li><b>Detention:</b> Supervised time spent at school outside of regular class hours.</li>
    <li><b>In-School Suspension (ISS):</b> Removal from the regular classroom setting to a designated area within the school.</li>
    <li><b>Out-of-School Suspension (OSS):</b> Temporary removal from the school premises.</li>
    <li><b>Expulsion:</b> Permanent removal from the school district (requires a formal hearing and superintendent approval).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Parent Tip:</b> Always ask for a written explanation of any disciplinary action taken against your child, regardless of its severity. This documentation is crucial for understanding the situation and protecting your child's rights.</p>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): Protecting Students with IEPs and 504 Plans</h2>

  <p>The IDEA provides specific protections for students with disabilities facing disciplinary action. A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a critical process designed to determine whether a student's misconduct is related to their disability or the school's failure to properly implement their IEP or 504 plan. According to 34 CFR § 300.530, an MDR is required when a student with an IEP or 504 plan is facing a suspension exceeding 10 consecutive school days or a series of suspensions that constitute a pattern of exclusion.</p>

  <p><b>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Notification:</b> The school must notify parents/guardians of the decision to suspend the student for more than 10 days and inform them of their right to an MDR.</li>
    <li><b>Gathering Information:</b> The MDR team, including parents/guardians, relevant school staff (e.g., special education teacher, general education teacher, school psychologist), and the student (when appropriate), reviews all relevant information in the student's file. This includes the IEP or 504 plan, teacher observations, disciplinary records, and any other relevant documentation.</li>
    <li><b>Determining Manifestation:</b> The team must answer two critical questions:
      <ul>
        <li>Was the conduct in question caused by or directly and substantially related to the child’s disability?</li>
        <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district’s failure to implement the IEP?</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Outcome:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>If the answer to either question is YES:</b> The conduct is determined to be a manifestation of the disability. The IEP team must then conduct a functional behavioral assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted, and implement or revise a behavior intervention plan (BIP) for the student. The student must be returned to their placement prior to the suspension, unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the BIP development.</li>
        <li><b>If the answer to both questions is NO:</b> The conduct is not a manifestation of the disability. The student can be disciplined in the same manner as a student without a disability, however, the school must still provide services to the student during the period of suspension so the student can continue to participate in the general education curriculum and advance toward achieving the goals set out in the student's IEP.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Actively participate in the MDR process. Bring relevant documentation, such as medical reports or independent evaluations, to support your position. Ask clarifying questions and ensure that the team considers all relevant information. If you disagree with the MDR outcome, you have the right to challenge the decision through due process.</p>

  <p><b>Common District Pitfalls in MDRs:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Insufficient Information:</b> The MDR team may not have access to all relevant information about the student's disability and its impact on their behavior.</li>
    <li><b>Pre-determined Outcomes:</b> The MDR may be conducted with a pre-determined outcome in mind, undermining its objectivity.</li>
    <li><b>Lack of Parent Involvement:</b> Parents may not be adequately informed about the MDR process or given sufficient opportunity to participate.</li>
    <li><b>Failure to Implement BIP:</b> Even if the conduct is deemed a manifestation, the school may fail to properly implement or revise the BIP, leading to further disciplinary issues.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Suspensions in Albany City SD: Your Rights and What to Do</h2>

  <p>Suspensions are a serious disciplinary action that can significantly disrupt a student's education. It is vital to understand your rights and the procedures involved in the suspension process.</p>

  <p><b>Your Rights During a Suspension:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Right to Notification:</b> You have the right to be notified in writing of the reasons for the suspension, the duration of the suspension, and your right to a hearing (for suspensions exceeding five days). This notification should comply with the requirements outlined in New York State Education Law § 3214.</li>
    <li><b>Right to a Hearing:</b> For suspensions exceeding five school days, you have the right to a formal hearing where you can present evidence and witnesses on your child's behalf.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Representation:</b> You have the right to be represented by an attorney or advocate at the hearing.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Appeal:</b> If you disagree with the suspension decision, you have the right to appeal to the superintendent (for suspensions up to five days) or the Board of Education (for suspensions exceeding five days).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Step-by-Step Guide: Responding to a Suspension</b></p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Immediately Request Documentation:</b> Request all documentation related to the suspension, including the incident report, witness statements, and any other evidence used to support the suspension decision.</li>
    <li><b>Prepare Your Child:</b> Talk to your child about the incident and allow them to explain their perspective. Help them understand the school's rules and expectations.</li>
    <li><b>Attend the Hearing (if applicable):</b> If your child is facing a suspension exceeding five days, prepare for the hearing. Gather evidence, identify witnesses, and consider seeking legal representation.</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep a detailed record of all communication with the school district, including dates, times, and the names of individuals you spoke with.</li>
    <li><b>Appeal the Decision (if necessary):</b> If you believe the suspension is unfair or discriminatory, file a timely appeal with the appropriate authority.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Don't be afraid to ask questions and challenge the school's decision if you believe it is unwarranted or violates your child's rights. Knowledge is power, and understanding your rights will empower you to advocate effectively for your child.</p>

  <h2>Due Process Rights in Albany City SD</h2>

  <p>Due process is a fundamental legal principle that ensures fairness and impartiality in decision-making. In the context of special education, due process provides parents with a mechanism to resolve disputes with the school district regarding the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of their child. 34 CFR § 300.507 outlines the due process procedures.</p>

  <p><b>When to Consider Due Process:</b></p>
  <ul>
    <li>The school district refuses to conduct an evaluation.</li>
    <li>You disagree with the results of the school district's evaluation.</li>
    <li>You disagree with the IEP developed for your child.</li>
    <li>The school district fails to implement your child's IEP.</li>
    <li>Disputes arise regarding disciplinary actions, including suspensions and expulsions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>The Due Process Complaint:</b></p>
  <p>The first step in initiating due process is filing a formal written complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must include specific information, such as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The name and address of the child.</li>
    <li>The name of the school.</li>
    <li>A description of the problem and the proposed resolution.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Resolution Meeting and Mediation:</b></p>
  <p>After filing the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution meeting with you within 15 days, unless you both agree to waive the meeting or use mediation. Mediation is a voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the school district reach a mutually agreeable solution.</p>

  <p><b>Due Process Hearing:</b></p>
  <p>If the resolution meeting or mediation does not resolve the dispute, you have the right to a due process hearing. The hearing is conducted before an impartial hearing officer (IHO) who is trained in special education law. You have the right to present evidence, call witnesses, and cross-examine the school district's witnesses. The IHO will issue a written decision within 45 days of the hearing.</p>

  <p><b>Appealing the Decision:</b></p>
  <p>If you disagree with the IHO's decision, you have the right to appeal to the State Review Officer (SRO) within 30 days.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

    <dl>
        <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between an IEP and a 504 plan?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> An IEP (Individualized Education Program) is for students with disabilities who require specialized instruction and related services to access the general education curriculum. A 504 plan is for students with disabilities who need accommodations and modifications to participate in school activities, but do not require specialized instruction.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: My child was suspended, but I wasn't notified. What should I do?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> Immediately contact the school principal and request written notification of the suspension, including the reasons for the suspension and your right to a hearing (if applicable). Document all communication with the school.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the school's decision to discipline my child?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> You have the right to appeal the decision. Follow the school district's procedures for appealing suspensions and disciplinary actions, and document all steps taken.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior that occurs off school grounds?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> In some cases, yes. If the off-campus behavior disrupts the school environment or poses a threat to the safety of students or staff, the school may take disciplinary action.</dd>

        <dt><b>Q: Where can I find the Albany City School District Code of Conduct?</b></dt>
        <dd><b>A:</b> The Albany City School District Code of Conduct is usually available on the district's official website. Search for "Albany City SD Code of Conduct" or contact your child's school for a copy.</dd>
    </dl>

  <p>For further details on student rights and the Albany City SD Code of Conduct, please refer to the district website or contact your school's administration.</p>
</body>
